  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1232 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThrough the remainder of this week, operational models still try to \nanalyze and further deepen a trough and associated mid-level low \nnear the Four Corners. Currently its moving southeast and is still \nexpected to go west and south of the Panhandles, so not expecting \nmuch in terms of impacts from it. Through the work week and into the \nweekend, we are still expecting dry and warm weather. \n\nEarly next week, global models are still showing signs of a \npotential weather pattern shift. A short-lived ridge builds back in \nbehind the trough that passes through this weekend, centered around \nthe TX/LA border, and extending north-northwest into the Plains. \nHowever, as it's building, a large trough off the coast of \nCalifornia tries to quickly push the ridge off to the east as it \nenters and travels through the Western US. This trough looks to dig \ndown into the Panhandles and interact with tropical moisture from a \nlandfalling tropical system off the Eastern Pacific. The ECMWF and \nGFS both have solutions containing this pattern, however, they are \nnot quite in agreement as to where the trough will ultimately set up \nduring the tropical moisture interaction. Given how far out this is, \na lot can change. We will continue to monitor the system over the \ncoming days. \n\nAbdullayev/Vanden Bosch",
